+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups, like `1h30m`. The units
+are `d`, `h`, `m`, and `s` for days, hours, minutes, and seconds.
+
+- Each unit may appear at most once, and units must follow the `d`, `h`, `m`,
+  `s` order. Units may be skipped, so `2h5s` is valid.
+- Units are case-insensitive: `45S` is the same as `45s`.
+- Amounts are non-negative integers. Signs, decimals, and spaces are rejected.
+- A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is returned
+  unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
